peru's political opposition is making  another attempt to oust president pedro  castillo he's been in office less than  eight months but opponents are trying  again to impeach him despite a failed  motion in december they're now feeling  confident  76 votes is more than we expected a good  sign a good start of the will of  legislators  but it may not be easy  to remove castillo from office the  political opposition needs 87 votes and  for that they need members of castillo's  own political party to turn against him  and for now that seems unlikely  however many peruvians are discontent 63  percent of the country disapproves of  his government overshadowed by  allegations of corruption at the highest  level including castillo himself but the  president refutes these allegations  ahead of impeachment proceedings  castillo requested to speak to lawmakers  tomorrow i'll be at the parliament the  first power of the state to tell them  what we've come to do what we're doing  and what we will do for this country  having reshuffled its cabinet four times  since taking office the government has  gone from one crisis to another  castillo's allies blame opponents for  the political instability  purpose from day one has been to bring  down castillo all roads lead to the  impeachment they lost the elections they  are very hurt and want to regain lost  ground  as castillo's defense or the president  himself prepared to face impeachment  proceedings in two weeks  legislators now raised to secure enough  votes to unseat him but many peruvians  angry at peru's political class say they  prefer the president and legislators  replaced  mariana sanchez  [Music]
 

